Q: WPF MVVM - How do I bind a radio button to a property A: <p>A simple solution is to use a <code>RadioButton.Command</code> and the <code>RadioButton.CommandParameter</code>.<br />
Alternatively, but with slightly more overhead, use a <code>Binding</code> or <code>MultiBinding</code> with a <code>IValueConverter</code>.<br />
Sometimes a <code>ItemsControl</code> where the <code>DataTemplate</code> is defined by the <code>RadioButton</code> (this can be useful if a group of <code>RadioButton</code> represents the state of data models you expose to the view) can make sense.</p>
<p>Also, you should not handle plain strings. Better define an <code>enum</code> e.g. <code>Gender</code> for more robust handling.<br />
That's of course optional but recommended.</p>
<p>So first step, no matter the chosen solution, is to introduce the enum:</p>
<p><strong>Gender.cs</strong></p>
<pre><code>public enum Gender
{
  Default = 0,
  Female,
  Male
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Then chose one of the following three solutions that suits your requirements best:</p>
<h2>1. <code>ICommand</code></h2>
<p>Good if you don't need two-way binding and only want to trigger an action based on the currently selected <code>RadioButton</code>.</p>
<p><strong>MainWindow.xaml</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;StackPanel&gt;
    &lt;RadioButton Content=&quot;Male&quot; 
                 Command=&quot;{Binding SetGenderCommand}&quot;
                 CommandParameter=&quot;{x:Static local:Gender.Male}&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;RadioButton Content=&quot;Female&quot;
                 Command=&quot;{Binding SetGenderCommand}&quot;
                 CommandParameter=&quot;{x:Static local:Gender.Female}&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/StackPanel&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>MainViewModel.cs</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>class MainViewModel : INotifyPropertyChanged
{
    public Gender Gender
    {
        get =&gt; _gender;
        set
        {
            if (!Enum.IsDefined(typeof(Gender), value))
            {
                throw new InvalidEnumArgumentException(
                    nameof(value),
                    (int)value,
                    typeof(Gender));
            }

            if (_gender == value)
            {
                return;
            }

            _gender = value;
            OnPropertyChanged();
        }
    }

    // Use your own ICommand implementation
    public ICommand SetGenderCommand =&gt; new RelayCommand(ExecuteSetGenderCommand);

    private void ExecuteSetGenderCommand(object commandParameter)
        =&gt; this.Gender = (Gender)commandParameter;

    public event PropertyChangedEventHandler? PropertyChanged;

    private void OnPropertyChanged([CallerMemberName] string? propertyName = null)
        =&gt; PropertyChanged?.Invoke(this, new PropertyChangedEventArgs(propertyName));
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>2. <code>IValueConverter</code></h2>
<p>Good when the selected value is state that should stay synchronized between the view model and the checked <code>RadioButton</code>.</p>
<p><strong>MainWindow.xaml</strong><br />
Set the <code>Binding.ConverterParameter</code> property to specify the role of the <code>RadioButton</code>. This is important later, when the converter must filter the value which maps to its role.</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;StackPanel&gt;
    &lt;StackPanel.Resources&gt;
        &lt;local:GenderToBooleanConverter x:Key=&quot;GenderToBooleanConverter&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/StackPanel.Resources&gt;

    &lt;RadioButton Content=&quot;Male&quot; 
                 IsChecked=&quot;{Binding SelectedGender, Converter={StaticResource GenderToBooleanConverter}, ConverterParameter={x:Static local:Gender.Male}}&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;RadioButton Content=&quot;Female&quot;
                 IsChecked=&quot;{Binding SelectedGender, Converter={StaticResource GenderToBooleanConverter}, ConverterParameter={x:Static local:Gender.Female}}&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/StackPanel&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>MainViewModel.cs</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>class MainViewModel : INotifyPropertyChanged
{
    private Gender _selectedGender;
    public Gender SelectedGender
    {
        get =&gt; _selectedGender;
        set
        {
            // Validate the enum since the value could be an invalid/undefined int
            if (!Enum.IsDefined&lt;Gender&gt;(value))
            {
                throw new InvalidEnumArgumentException(
                    &quot;value&quot;, 
                    (int)value, 
                    typeof(Gender));
            }

            _selectedGender = value;
            OnPropertyChanged();
        }
    }

    public event PropertyChangedEventHandler? PropertyChanged;

    private void OnPropertyChanged([CallerMemberName] string? propertyName = null)
        =&gt; PropertyChanged?.Invoke(this, new PropertyChangedEventArgs(propertyName));
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>GenderToBooleanConverter.cs</strong><br />
The converter includes parameter validation and is specialized for enums.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class GenderToBooleanConverter : IValueConverter
{
    // Get current Gender from view model (binding source).
    public object Convert(object value, Type targetType, object parameter, CultureInfo culture)
    {
        Gender radioButtonGender = GetGenderParameter(parameter);

        // Return true if the source value equals the value represented by this RadioButton.
        return value is Gender selectedGender
            &amp;&amp; selectedGender == radioButtonGender;
    }

    // Send selected Gender from RadioButton back to view model (binding source).
    public object ConvertBack(object value, Type targetType, object parameter, CultureInfo culture)
    {
        Gender radioButtonGender = GetGenderParameter(parameter);

        // Do nothing when:
        // - the target value is not a bool
        // - the RadioButton is unchecked, so it must not overwrite the currently selected value.
        if (value is not bool isChecked || !isChecked)
        {
            return Binding.DoNothing;
        }

        return radioButtonGender;
    }

    private static Gender GetGenderParameter(object parameter)
    {
        if (parameter is not Gender gender)
        {
            throw new ArgumentException(
                $&quot;The converter parameter must be of type '{typeof(Gender).FullName}'.&quot;,
                nameof(parameter));
        }

        // Note that Enum.IsDefined() this is not reliable for [Flags] enums
        if (!Enum.IsDefined(typeof(Gender), gender))
        {
            throw new InvalidEnumArgumentException(
                nameof(parameter),
                (int)gender,
                typeof(Gender));
        }

        return gender;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>3. <code>ListBox</code></h2>
<p>Good if the available options should be represented by data models and you want the selected option to be stored directly as a property on the view model.</p>
<p>This solution uses a <code>ListBox</code>, since it introduces the <code>SelectedItem</code> property so that the data model itself must not track selection state. The <code>ListBox</code> changes its template so that it looks like a simple <code>ItemsControl</code>. The <code>ListBox.SelectedItem</code> property is bound to the selected option on the view model. Each item is displayed as a <code>RadioButton</code>.</p>
<p><strong>MainWindow.xaml</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;ListBox ItemsSource=&quot;{Binding GenderOptions}&quot;
         SelectedItem=&quot;{Binding SelectedGender}&quot;
         SelectionMode=&quot;Single&quot;
         BorderThickness=&quot;0&quot;
         Background=&quot;Transparent&quot;&gt;
    &lt;ListBox.Template&gt;
        &lt;ControlTemplate TargetType=&quot;{x:Type ListBox}&quot;&gt;
            &lt;!-- No ScrollViewer: this makes the ListBox behave visually more like an ItemsControl. --&gt;
            &lt;ItemsPresenter /&gt;
        &lt;/ControlTemplate&gt;
    &lt;/ListBox.Template&gt;

    &lt;ListBox.ItemContainerStyle&gt;
        &lt;Style TargetType=&quot;{x:Type ListBoxItem}&quot;&gt;
            &lt;Setter Property=&quot;Padding&quot; Value=&quot;0&quot; /&gt;
            &lt;Setter Property=&quot;Margin&quot; Value=&quot;0&quot; /&gt;
            &lt;Setter Property=&quot;HorizontalContentAlignment&quot; Value=&quot;Stretch&quot; /&gt;

            &lt;!-- Remove the default ListBoxItem selection visuals. 
                 The RadioButton will show the selected state instead. --&gt;
            &lt;Setter Property=&quot;Template&quot;&gt;
                &lt;Setter.Value&gt;
                    &lt;ControlTemplate TargetType=&quot;{x:Type ListBoxItem}&quot;&gt;
                        &lt;ContentPresenter /&gt;
                    &lt;/ControlTemplate&gt;
                &lt;/Setter.Value&gt;
            &lt;/Setter&gt;
        &lt;/Style&gt;
    &lt;/ListBox.ItemContainerStyle&gt;

    &lt;ListBox.ItemTemplate&gt;
        &lt;DataTemplate DataType=&quot;{x:Type local:GenderOption}&quot;&gt;
            &lt;RadioButton Content=&quot;{Binding DisplayName}&quot;
                         IsChecked=&quot;{Binding IsSelected,
                                             RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ListBoxItem}},
                                             Mode=TwoWay}&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;/DataTemplate&gt;
    &lt;/ListBox.ItemTemplate&gt;
&lt;/ListBox&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>GenderOption.cs</strong><br />
The data model to represent (or *contain *, in case the data model is more complex) the <code>Gender</code> option.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public sealed class GenderOption
{
    public GenderOption(Gender gender, string displayName)
    {
        if (!Enum.IsDefined(typeof(Gender), gender))
        {
            throw new InvalidEnumArgumentException(
                nameof(gender),
                (int)gender,
                typeof(Gender));
        }

        Gender = gender;
        DisplayName = displayName;
    }

    public Gender Gender { get; }
    public string DisplayName { get; }
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>MainViewModel.cs</strong></p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public sealed class MainViewModel : INotifyPropertyChanged
{
    private GenderOption _selectedGender;

    public MainViewModel()
    {
        GenderOptions =
        [
            new GenderOption(Gender.Default, &quot;Not specified&quot;),
            new GenderOption(Gender.Female, &quot;Female&quot;),
            new GenderOption(Gender.Male, &quot;Male&quot;)
        ];

        _selectedGender = GenderOptions[0];
    }

    public ObservableCollection&lt;GenderOption&gt; GenderOptions { get; }

    public GenderOption SelectedGender
    {
        get =&gt; _selectedGender;
        set
        {
            if (value is null)
            {
                throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(value));
            }

            if (ReferenceEquals(_selectedGender, value))
            {
                return;
            }

            _selectedGender = value;
            OnPropertyChanged();
            OnPropertyChanged(nameof(Gender));
        }
    }

    public Gender Gender =&gt; SelectedGender.Gender;

    public event PropertyChangedEventHandler? PropertyChanged;

    private void OnPropertyChanged([CallerMemberName] string? propertyName = null)
        =&gt; PropertyChanged?.Invoke(this, new PropertyChangedEventArgs(propertyName));
}
</code></pre>

Q: Catched NodeJS Errors still propagate? A: <p>Not a complete answer to my problem and I don't understand why, but I could fix my issue by chaining <code>then</code> and <code>catch</code>. Compare these examples:</p>
<h2>Failing</h2>
<pre><code>(async () =&gt; {
  // The async generator
  const computer = async function *run() {
    throw Error('Some error!')
  }

  // Initialize the generator
  const runner = computer()

  // Store reference to the promise
  const promise = runner.next()

  // Handle success
  promise.then(() =&gt; {
    console.log(&quot;Handle success&quot;)
  })

  // Handle fail    
  promise.catch((error: Error) =&gt; {
    console.log(&quot;Catch&quot;, error)
  });

  // Await Error/Success
  await promise;
})();
</code></pre>
<h2>Succeeding</h2>
<pre><code>(async () =&gt; {
  // The async generator
  const computer = async function *run() {
    throw Error('Some error!')
  }

  // Initialize the generator
  const runner = computer()

  // Use chaining!
  const promise = runner.next()
    .then(() =&gt; {
      console.log(&quot;Handle success&quot;)
    })
    .catch((error: Error) =&gt; {
      console.log(&quot;Catch&quot;, error)
    });

  // Await Error/Success
  await promise;
})();
</code></pre>

Q: How to get video tags from a Youtube video? A: <p>YouTube removed tags from the public video page a few years ago, so you can no longer extract them by scraping the HTML directly. There are two reliable ways to get them now.</p>
<p><strong>Option 1: YouTube Data API v3</strong></p>
<p>This is the official method. You need an API key from Google Cloud Console and the video ID.</p>
<pre><code>$apiKey = 'YOUR_API_KEY';
$videoId = 'VIDEO_ID';

$url = &quot;https://www.googleapis.com/youtube/v3/videos?part=snippet&amp;id={$videoId}&amp;key={$apiKey}&quot;;

$response = file_get_contents($url);
$data = json_decode($response, true);

$tags = $data['items'][0]['snippet']['tags'];
print_r($tags);
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>tags</code> field is inside the <code>snippet</code> part of the response. Keep in mind that if the video owner has not added any tags, the field will not appear in the response at all.</p>
<p><strong>Option 2: No-code approach</strong></p>
<p>If you just need to extract tags from a video without setting up API access, there are free tools that handle this without any code. YT Description Extractor at ytdescriptionextractor.com pulls tags, descriptions, thumbnails and transcripts from any YouTube URL in one click.</p>
